{
  "evidence": "TEXT_A: End NHS privatisation with +£8bn in year one rising to £28bn by 2030; universal NHS dentistry; mental health therapies within 28 days and a counsellor in every school; free personal care funded at £20bn/year; raise Universal Credit by £40/week; scrap the two‑child cap; disability benefits +5%; universal free school meals; Clean Air Act plus large-scale home insulation and low‑carbon heat; rent controls and ending no‑fault evictions; 10:1 pay ratio cap. TEXT_B: Freeze non‑essential visas; detain and deport illegal entrants and leave the ECHR if needed; 40,000 extra police, zero‑tolerance policing and more stop and search; mandatory life for repeat violent offenders; stronger welfare work requirements; 20% tax relief on private healthcare/insurance and patient vouchers; scrap Net Zero and fast‑track North Sea and shale; a ‘patriotic’ curriculum and ban ‘transgender ideology’ in schools; replace the Equalities Act and introduce a British Bill of Rights.",
  "reasoning": "Given the profile’s strongest weight on Care, Text A’s extensive commitments to expand and fund health, social care, welfare, clean air and housing protections align more closely than Text B’s emphasis on enforcement, immigration crackdowns, welfare tightening and market mechanisms in healthcare. With only moderate Authority and low Sanctity in the profile, Text B’s heavy law‑and‑order, sovereignty and cultural‑purity agenda overshoots those values, whereas Text A’s lighter authority stance and stronger equality measures better fit the modest equality and restrained authority preferences.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "A"
}
